"# Appendix O — The Wrong Sink

Chapter 8 taught you to treat a single error as *data* — a value you can
catch, inspect, and branch on. This appendix zooms out from one error to
the whole population of them, and shows the error census as an
introspection mechanism with an unusual purpose. Most introspection
answers \"what is this, and how does it work?\" The census answers a
darker, more useful question: \"what kinds of wrong keep happening, and
which ones are worth steering away from?\"

## O.1 A sink for wrongs, not a ranker of rights

The error census is a WRONG SINK. Every failure that escapes a running
program drains into it, is normalized to a stable key, and is counted.
Read that sentence twice, because the emphasis is on *wrong*. The
census does not rank good answers. It has no notion of a right answer at
all. Its entire job is NEGATIVE SELECTION — to accumulate the shapes
that fail, so that failing shapes can be recognized and avoided.

This is a deliberate design choice, not an omission. You could imagine a
system that scored candidate answers and picked the best one. Motoi does
not build that. The reasoning: picking the *best* right answer is what
the model's own intelligence is for. What the model cannot easily do
from the inside is know, across thousands of runs, which specific wrong
shapes recur. So the census takes on exactly that job — eliminate the
wrongs — and leaves the choosing-among-survivors to the model. Negative
selection. Not a right-ranker; a wrong-sink.

## O.2 Match-or-mint: how a wrong becomes a coordinate

The first thing the census does with a fresh failure is normalize it.
An ugly host error and a clean Scheme error both collapse onto a small,
stable set of ERROR-KEYS. A brand-new key is *minted* the first time it
is seen; a recurring key *matches* an existing coordinate. That is
match-or-mint.

A minted key starts life as `provisional` — one data point is a fluke,
not a pattern. Watch it earn promotion.

The census is *persistent process state*: it lives in a stats file, and
every escaped error in every run drains into the same file. So to watch
one key's life cleanly you must give it a census of its own. Point
`MOTOI_STATS_PATH` at a scratch file and the experiment is reproducible.
Put `(no-such-verb 1)` in `once.scm` and `(display (stats/error-counts 3))`
in `read.scm`, then:

```text
$ MOTOI_STATS_PATH=/tmp/census.slat ./bin/motoi run once.scm
error: unbound symbol: no-such-verb

$ MOTOI_STATS_PATH=/tmp/census.slat ./bin/motoi run read.scm
((:unbound-symbol 1 provisional))
```

One occurrence, `provisional`. Now let it recur past the promote
threshold (which is three):

```text
$ MOTOI_STATS_PATH=/tmp/census.slat ./bin/motoi run once.scm
error: unbound symbol: no-such-verb

$ MOTOI_STATS_PATH=/tmp/census.slat ./bin/motoi run once.scm
error: unbound symbol: no-such-verb

$ MOTOI_STATS_PATH=/tmp/census.slat ./bin/motoi run read.scm
((:unbound-symbol 3 real))
```

Note that each occurrence needed its own process. The census records
errors that *escape* — an error you catch with `guard` never reaches the
sink, because from the program's point of view nothing went wrong.

At the third occurrence it flips to `real`. The status is the census
deciding, on its own, which wrongs are *load-bearing* — recurring shapes
worth building a lesson around — versus which are one-off noise. This is
introspection with an opinion: the system watches its own failures and
promotes the ones that matter.

## O.3 Read deltas and ratios, not absolutes

Here is the introspection discipline that separates a novice reader of
the census from a fluent one. An absolute count is almost meaningless on
its own. \"Eleven unbound symbols\" — is that a lot? Compared to what?
The signal lives in *relationships*: this count versus the total, this
session versus lifetime, this count now versus this count before a fix.

The ratio is the most useful shape. Build a helper that reports one
key's share of all failures:

```scheme
(define (error-share key)
  (let ((total (cadr (assoc :total-errors (stats/error-summary))))
        (c (stats/error-count-of key)))
    (if (> total 0)
        (exact->inexact (/ c total))
        0)))
```

Against a scratch census holding two `unbound-symbol` failures and one
`not-a-pair`:

```text
$ MOTOI_STATS_PATH=/tmp/census2.slat ./bin/motoi run share.scm
0.6666666666666666
```

Two-thirds of everything that ever went wrong was an unbound symbol.
*That* is a finding, in a way that a raw `2` never is. It says: the
single most valuable thing this system could do for its users is catch
typos and missing imports before they run. The census turned a pile of
failures into a priority — but only once you read it as a ratio.

Deltas matter just as much. The `stats/error-summary` alist separates
lifetime from session so you can watch change over time:

```text
$ MOTOI_STATS_PATH=/tmp/census2.slat ./bin/motoi run summary.scm
((:distinct-errors 2) (:total-errors 3) (:real 0)
 (:provisional 2) (:novel-this-session 0)
 (:errors-this-session 0)
 (:stats-slat /tmp/census2.slat))
```

`:novel-this-session` and `:errors-this-session` are the mint side of
match-or-mint, scoped to right now. A wrong that is novel this session
is one the census has never minted before — the leading edge of a new
failure shape. Reading it as a delta against the lifetime totals is how
you notice a *new* kind of wrong appearing, as opposed to an old one
recurring.

## O.4 Closing the loop: count to card to graded fix

A count by itself is inert. The census closes into action through the
error-card catalogue at `scheme/reference/error-cards/`. The loop:

1. **Count.** The census surfaces a key and a rising count — say
   `unbound-symbol` is now `real` and holds two-thirds of all failures.
2. **Card.** Retrieve the matching card. It explains what that wrong
   *means*, how to recognize it at a verb, and the honest fix shape.
   When the census promotes a fresh key to `real`, that is the signal a
   new card is owed; the mint script scaffolds a stub for a human (or
   the model) to fill. Boot the shelf at `error-cards/README.slatl`.
3. **Fix, graded by the interpreter.** Apply the fix and re-run. The
   grader is not a rubric — it is the interpreter itself. A fix that
   landed makes the wrong stop escaping; a fix that did not makes the
   count keep climbing. The same runtime that recorded the wrong grades
   the repair, by whether the wrong recurs.

That is the whole introspection cycle: the system observes its own
failures, names them, teaches around the recurring ones, and lets the
interpreter certify the repairs by their absence. Negative selection all
the way down — the census never tells the model what to *do*; it only
drains away what not to.

## O.5 Why negative selection is the right shape for a small model

A small model writing Scheme does not need a scoreboard of good answers
ranked for it; ranking is what its own intelligence does well. What it
lacks is *memory of its own recurring wrongs* across sessions it cannot
remember. The census is exactly that memory, and it is deliberately
one-sided: it holds only the wrongs, promotes only the recurring ones,
and grades fixes only by disappearance. The model keeps its diversity —
many right ways survive — while the sink steadily removes the wrong ways
that keep coming back.

Read this way, the error census is not a debugging convenience bolted on
the side. It is introspection in its purest negative form: a program
learning what it should stop doing, by watching what it keeps doing
wrong, and letting the interpreter be the judge.
"
)
